-- title:
--   Corollary 3.12: Pointed cone criterion using both Jordan components
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g$ be reductive and $x=x_s+x_n$ a commuting ad-semisimple/ad-nilpotent Jordan decomposition with $x_n\in[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]$. Here $x_s$ includes the central part. Then
--
--   $$C_x\text{ is pointed}\iff\overline{C_{x_n}+C_{x_s}}\text{ is pointed}.$$
--
--   The closure is over the entire sum, as displayed in the PDF. This is a pointedness test using both components.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 11, Corollary 3.12: Pointed cone criterion using both Jordan components

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Frontier30_P26

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Frontier30.P26

noncomputable section

